00:02:21.611 --> 00:02:22.529
A story:

31
00:02:22.809 --> 00:02:25.134
400 questions and no answers.

32
00:02:25.414 --> 00:02:31.729
Arjun collected four hundred system design questions from forums, books and friends into a spreadsheet.

33
00:02:32.009 --> 00:02:34.547
He read through it twice in the week before his loop.

34
00:02:34.827 --> 00:02:39.478
In the interview he recognised every question, and could answer none of them fluently.

35
00:02:39.758 --> 00:02:46.777
He knew about consistent hashing, but couldn't explain in thirty seconds why virtual nodes help, or what he'd trade for them.

36
00:02:47.057 --> 00:02:50.513
Recognition is not recall, and recall is not fluency.

37
00:02:50.793 --> 00:02:59.241
A question bank is useful only if you: answer aloud before reading;. answer in a fixed shape (30 seconds, then depth, then trade-offs);.

38
00:03:00.591 --> 00:03:05.113
expect the follow-ups;. space the practice so the answers are still there on the day.

39
00:03:05.393 --> 00:03:08.188
This chapter turns the handbook into that kind of bank.

