543
00:24:46.577 --> 00:24:47.515
Requirement:

544
00:24:47.795 --> 00:24:51.529
Remember the answers on interview day, and still know them a month later.

545
00:24:52.879 --> 00:24:55.759
Here is the Java code, SpacedL4.java.

546
00:24:56.039 --> 00:24:59.772
It runs to 87 lines; the full listing is in the chapter.

547
00:25:00.052 --> 00:25:07.199
SpacedL4.java, run with: java SpacedL4.java (JDK 21, no dependencies).

548
00:25:07.479 --> 00:25:10.764
HOW TO STUDY 415 QUESTIONS IN SIX WEEKS.

549
00:25:11.044 --> 00:25:23.076
A simple memory model (an assumption, in the spirit of spaced-repetition. research, VERIFY against your own experience): after a review, the chance of recalling an answer t days later is.

550
00:25:25.926 --> 00:25:26.843
On screen:

551
00:25:27.123 --> 00:25:46.387
415 cards, ~40 reviews per day budget, interview on day 43; recall(t) = exp(minus t or S), S times 2.5. on success. plan expected recall cards 80% recall 30 days reviews. on interview day that day. later (2nd loop).

552
00:25:46.667 --> 00:25:56.929
P1) cram: all 415 on each of the last 4 days 61% 143 3% 1660.

553
00:25:57.209 --> 00:25:58.681
P2) round-robin:

554
00:25:58.961 --> 00:26:04.593
40 a day in a fixed cycle 6% 0 0% 1680.

555
00:26:04.873 --> 00:26:13.491
P3) spaced: due cards first, 15 new a day 24% 87 11% 1645.

556
00:26:13.771 --> 00:26:31.627
P4) spaced (days 1 to 40) + all 415 on the last 2 days 57% 86 17% 2395. then under this model, cramming wins the next morning and loses a month later: it. fills short-term memory.

557
00:26:31.907 --> 00:26:33.422
The rest is in the chapter.

558
00:26:36.272 --> 00:26:37.125
Reading it:

559
00:26:37.405 --> 00:26:39.923
Cramming wins the next morning and fades fast.

560
00:26:40.203 --> 00:26:42.293
A fixed cycle is the worst of both.

561
00:26:42.573 --> 00:26:47.907
Spaced review plus a final pass is close to cramming on the day and best a month later.

562
00:26:48.187 --> 00:26:50.576
The model is deliberately simple and harsh.

563
00:26:50.856 --> 00:26:53.224
Trust the direction, not the percentages.

