660
00:29:50.373 --> 00:29:51.248
Part 8.

661
00:29:51.528 --> 00:29:54.216
Interview Q and A, Integration Questions.

662
00:29:55.466 --> 00:29:58.026
These questions deliberately cross chapters.

663
00:29:58.306 --> 00:30:02.807
They are the kind senior and staff interviewers use to test whether knowledge connects.

664
00:30:04.157 --> 00:30:04.989
Beginner.

665
00:30:05.269 --> 00:30:06.293
Question 1.

666
00:30:06.573 --> 00:30:08.067
A product page is slow.

667
00:30:08.347 --> 00:30:09.968
Walk through where the time could go.

668
00:30:10.248 --> 00:30:11.613
Thirty-second answer:

669
00:30:11.893 --> 00:30:26.592
DNS or TLS and network to the edge, CDN miss to origin, API gateway, service latency (DB queries, cache misses, downstream calls), payload size and rendering; measure with tracing before guessing.

670
00:30:27.942 --> 00:30:28.945
Question 2.

671
00:30:29.225 --> 00:30:31.699
Why do we need both a database and a cache?

672
00:30:31.979 --> 00:30:33.345
Thirty-second answer:

673
00:30:33.625 --> 00:30:44.441
The database is the durable source of truth with transactions; the cache serves hot reads faster and cheaper; together they trade freshness and complexity for latency and load.

674
00:30:45.791 --> 00:30:46.836
Question 3.

675
00:30:47.116 --> 00:30:49.591
What happens when you place an order, end to end?

676
00:30:49.871 --> 00:30:51.236
Thirty-second answer:

677
00:30:51.516 --> 00:31:04.231
The app calls the gateway; checkout validates price and serviceability, reserves stock, holds a slot, charges payment, creates the order; events trigger picking, dispatch, notifications and invoices.

678
00:31:05.581 --> 00:31:06.647
Question 4.

679
00:31:06.927 --> 00:31:09.978
What's the difference between scaling reads and scaling writes?

680
00:31:10.258 --> 00:31:11.623
Thirty-second answer:

681
00:31:11.903 --> 00:31:20.906
Reads scale with replicas and caches; writes need partitioning (sharding), batching, or async processing because a single primary bounds them.

682
00:31:22.256 --> 00:31:23.259
Intermediate.

683
00:31:23.539 --> 00:31:24.648
Question 5.

684
00:31:24.928 --> 00:31:28.256
How do idempotency, retries and timeouts fit together?

685
00:31:28.536 --> 00:31:29.901
Thirty-second answer:

686
00:31:30.181 --> 00:31:44.773
Timeouts bound waiting; retries recover transient failures; idempotency makes retries safe because the operation may have succeeded; add backoff with jitter and treat timeouts on side-effecting calls as unknown outcomes.

687
00:31:46.123 --> 00:31:47.233
Question 6.

688
00:31:47.513 --> 00:31:50.243
How do the outbox pattern and Kafka work together?

689
00:31:50.523 --> 00:31:51.889
Thirty-second answer:

690
00:31:52.169 --> 00:32:05.395
Write the business change and an outbox row in one local transaction; a relay (polling or CDC) publishes outbox rows to Kafka; consumers are idempotent because delivery is at-least-once.

691
00:32:06.745 --> 00:32:07.833
Question 7.

692
00:32:08.113 --> 00:32:10.993
How does caching interact with inventory correctness?

693
00:32:11.273 --> 00:32:12.639
Thirty-second answer:

694
00:32:12.919 --> 00:32:24.225
Browse can show cached, slightly stale availability; the checkout path must check and reserve against the source of truth atomically; show few left hints rather than exact counts from cache.

695
00:32:25.575 --> 00:32:26.599
Question 8.

696
00:32:26.879 --> 00:32:29.653
How do SLOs, alerts and dashboards relate?

697
00:32:29.933 --> 00:32:31.298
Thirty-second answer:

698
00:32:31.578 --> 00:32:42.309
SLOs define reliability targets from user journeys; alerts page on error-budget burn rates; dashboards support diagnosis; each alert should link to a runbook.

699
00:32:43.659 --> 00:32:44.725
Question 9.

700
00:32:45.005 --> 00:32:47.907
How do schema changes and deployments interact?

701
00:32:48.187 --> 00:32:49.552
Thirty-second answer:

702
00:32:49.832 --> 00:33:03.784
Old and new code run together during rollouts, so schema changes must be backward compatible: expand (add), migrate, switch reads or writes, contract (remove), each deploy compatible with the previous version.

703
00:33:05.134 --> 00:33:05.902
Senior.

704
00:33:06.182 --> 00:33:07.227
Question 10.

705
00:33:07.507 --> 00:33:11.006
Walk a payment through every consistency mechanism it touches.

706
00:33:11.286 --> 00:33:12.651
Thirty-second answer:

707
00:33:12.931 --> 00:33:28.931
Idempotency key at the API; state machine with guarded transitions in the DB; outbox to events; webhook dedup and forward-only updates; status polling for unknowns; double-entry ledger; daily reconciliation with settlement files.

708
00:33:30.281 --> 00:33:31.412
Question 11.

709
00:33:31.692 --> 00:33:36.172
How do partitioning, replication and consensus fit together in one store?

710
00:33:36.452 --> 00:33:37.817
Thirty-second answer:

711
00:33:38.097 --> 00:33:55.953
Partitioning spreads data (consistent hashing or ranges); replication copies each partition (leader-based or leaderless); consensus (Raft or Paxos) decides leaders and ordered logs where strong consistency is needed; membership and failure detection glue it together.

712
00:33:57.303 --> 00:33:58.391
Question 12.

713
00:33:58.671 --> 00:34:01.466
How would you make the platform survive a Kafka outage?

714
00:34:01.746 --> 00:34:03.111
Thirty-second answer:

715
00:34:03.391 --> 00:34:20.970
Outboxes keep producers safe (events wait in the DB); critical paths don't depend synchronously on Kafka; consumers catch up after recovery; monitor outbox depth; for prolonged outages degrade features that rely on events (for example, delayed notifications).

716
00:34:22.320 --> 00:34:23.600
Question 13.

717
00:34:23.880 --> 00:34:27.357
How do security and multi-tenancy intersect for sellers?

718
00:34:27.637 --> 00:34:29.003
Thirty-second answer:

719
00:34:29.283 --> 00:34:44.216
Tenant identity in tokens, row-level or schema isolation enforced in the data layer, per-tenant rate limits and quotas, encryption keys per tenant where needed, and audit trails, isolation for data, performance and blast radius.

720
00:34:45.566 --> 00:34:46.761
Question 14.

721
00:34:47.041 --> 00:34:51.585
How do performance testing and capacity planning work together for sale day?

722
00:34:51.865 --> 00:34:53.230
Thirty-second answer:

723
00:34:53.510 --> 00:35:06.651
Forecast journeys' peak load, find per-service capacity by load tests with realistic data and failures, compute headroom and pre-scaling, validate with a full-journey test, and set admission control for the excess.

724
00:35:08.001 --> 00:35:08.897
Architect.

725
00:35:09.177 --> 00:35:10.372
Question 15.

726
00:35:10.652 --> 00:35:14.535
Connect DDD, team topology and microservice boundaries.

727
00:35:14.815 --> 00:35:16.180
Thirty-second answer:

728
00:35:16.460 --> 00:35:33.697
Bounded contexts define language and consistency boundaries; teams own contexts end to end (Conway); services follow contexts where independent deployment and scaling pay off; start as modules and extract when load, team size or change rate demands.

729
00:35:35.047 --> 00:35:36.242
Question 16.

730
00:35:36.522 --> 00:35:40.831
How do cost, reliability and compliance trade off in data residency?

731
00:35:41.111 --> 00:35:42.477
Thirty-second answer:

732
00:35:42.757 --> 00:35:59.589
Residency limits where data and replicas live, which constrains DR regions and CDN or processing choices, raising cost; classify data, keep regulated data in-country with in-country DR, and use global services only for non-regulated data.

733
00:36:00.939 --> 00:36:02.283
Question 17.

734
00:36:02.563 --> 00:36:07.960
What architecture would you choose for 10k orders per day versus 1M orders per day, and why?

735
00:36:08.240 --> 00:36:09.605
Thirty-second answer:

736
00:36:09.885 --> 00:36:17.480
10k per day: a modular monolith, one PostgreSQL, managed queue, CDN, fast to build and operate.

737
00:36:17.760 --> 00:36:30.219
1M per day: extracted checkout or payments, search, dispatch; event backbone; read models; cells and DR for the critical set, justified by load, team size and blast radius.

738
00:36:31.569 --> 00:36:32.742
Question 18.

739
00:36:33.022 --> 00:36:37.182
How do you decide which consistency model each part of a platform needs?

740
00:36:37.462 --> 00:36:38.827
Thirty-second answer:

741
00:36:39.107 --> 00:36:57.049
By the cost of anomalies: money and scarce stock need linearizable or serializable operations on their critical keys; carts need read-your-writes; catalog, search and feeds tolerate staleness; analytics tolerate approximation, encoded per operation, not per system.

742
00:36:58.399 --> 00:36:59.636
Question 19.

743
00:36:59.916 --> 00:37:05.761
Where do most production incidents in such platforms actually come from, and how does design reduce them?

744
00:37:06.041 --> 00:37:07.407
Thirty-second answer:

745
00:37:07.687 --> 00:37:26.183
Changes (deploys, config, schema), overload and retry storms, dependency failures, and data drift; design reduces them with safe rollouts, backpressure and budgets, soft dependencies and degradation, idempotency with reconciliation, and cells to bound blast radius.

746
00:37:27.533 --> 00:37:28.642
Question 20.

747
00:37:28.922 --> 00:37:32.741
If you had to teach one principle from this whole handbook, what would it be?

748
00:37:33.021 --> 00:37:34.386
Thirty-second answer:

749
00:37:34.666 --> 00:37:49.919
Make guarantees explicit and verify them: every design choice exists to keep some invariant true under some failure, so name the invariant, the failure, the mechanism, and the check that proves it, the labs in this handbook are that principle applied.

